you're not sure you have the right org.

00:37:53.110 --> 00:37:55.720
So, so, so that's- so dig into that a little bit, uh,

00:37:55.720 --> 00:37:59.065
um, one of the things that we look for it- it's basically habit.

00:37:59.065 --> 00:38:01.360
So you, you start using SOPs to do

00:38:01.360 --> 00:38:03.745
the exact same thing and that's great when you're doing the right thing.

00:38:03.745 --> 00:38:04.060
Yup.

00:38:04.060 --> 00:38:05.980
But when you're doing the, the, what is it?

00:38:05.980 --> 00:38:08.395
The wrong thing the right way and you can go down really quick.

00:38:08.395 --> 00:38:08.860
Yup.

00:38:08.860 --> 00:38:11.575
Uh, any red flags you look for that people are just

00:38:11.575 --> 00:38:14.890
doing what they've always done because they've always done it and you gotta,

00:38:14.890 --> 00:38:16.450
you gotta put the brakes?

00:38:16.450 --> 00:38:18.100
Yes. So that the- the, I mean,

00:38:18.100 --> 00:38:20.650
the red flag is when you get people not asking questions.

00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:22.180
Silence is like ohm.

00:38:22.180 --> 00:38:22.720
Yeah.

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:23.340
[LAUGHTER].

00:38:23.340 --> 00:38:26.230
The, um, so what I- the very early days of data science in

00:38:26.230 --> 00:38:28.360
2005 and we bought it- somebody

00:38:28.360 --> 00:38:30.985
before it's called data science into Mozilla which is we had like,

00:38:30.985 --> 00:38:33.940
you know, 100 million users on which you couldn't tell what's happening at the user-base.

00:38:33.940 --> 00:38:36.220
So Robert Diaz, the data scientist who worked for

00:38:36.220 --> 00:38:39.865
Steve Levitt on Freakonomics [NOISE] in- and,

00:38:39.865 --> 00:38:42.580
um, so he just started generating reports and

00:38:42.580 --> 00:38:45.985
I had him send it to me every night at seven o'clock.

00:38:45.985 --> 00:38:51.235
And my executive team didn't like this very much because here I was,

00:38:51.235 --> 00:38:54.505
just got home, having dinner and they would see this [OVERLAPPING] report.

00:38:54.505 --> 00:38:58.090
They'd see this report coming through and like at 7:03 like there'll be

00:38:58.090 --> 00:38:59.980
me responding like why is

00:38:59.980 --> 00:39:02.350
this one like this and marked down in Germany and why is this like this.

00:39:02.350 --> 00:39:04.390
And I was trying to teach people like every time,

00:39:04.390 --> 00:39:06.100
every day, look at things and say,

00:39:06.100 --> 00:39:07.960
"Oh, why does it look like this? What's happening?"

00:39:07.960 --> 00:39:08.680
No. That's true. Question.

00:39:08.680 --> 00:39:10.180
Yeah. People didn't love it because they,

00:39:10.180 --> 00:39:12.235
they felt like they had to answer the questions.

00:39:12.235 --> 00:39:14.140
But it got better over time.

00:39:14.140 --> 00:39:15.160
They started asking questions.

00:39:15.160 --> 00:39:17.650
And send out, send out three or send out two before acting.

00:39:17.650 --> 00:39:21.850
Yeah. I want to sort of step in here and invite our viewers to send more questions.

