Um, [NOISE] you know, a lot of- a lot of

00:40:05.560 --> 00:40:07.900
companies today will- don't want to hire directors

00:40:07.900 --> 00:40:10.030
or VPs and they'll still call everybody a head-up or

00:40:10.030 --> 00:40:12.865
something to indicate leadership and ownership.

00:40:12.865 --> 00:40:16.780
Um, [NOISE] I- I think this is a challenging question.

00:40:16.780 --> 00:40:18.760
I don't- I don't really have a good answer, too.

00:40:18.760 --> 00:40:21.595
I think that you should be clear about who's on your executive team.

00:40:21.595 --> 00:40:23.560
That you should be clear about ownership and

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:26.390
I need- to the extent you can't be usually clear about,

00:40:26.460 --> 00:40:30.460
um, [NOISE] ah, compensation progression.

00:40:30.460 --> 00:40:31.240
Right.

00:40:31.240 --> 00:40:34.195
So having five or six levels, you will progress through.

00:40:34.195 --> 00:40:38.035
A [inaudible] levels almost always should be the force between titles.

00:40:38.035 --> 00:40:55.300
Right.

00:40:55.300 --> 00:40:55.750
Yes. [NOISE]

00:40:55.750 --> 00:40:57.190
But- [NOISE] [inaudible] This is quite right. Extending them

00:40:57.190 --> 00:40:58.615
to where the decision rights are unclear.

00:40:58.615 --> 00:41:01.390
What- what that leads to is a bunch of dysfunctional conflict where people act

00:41:01.390 --> 00:41:04.240
like cats scratching for, um, to the top.

00:41:04.240 --> 00:41:06.310
So, uh, I think that's the most important thing.

00:41:06.310 --> 00:41:07.990
I think the [inaudible] idea is to-

00:41:07.990 --> 00:41:09.700
To idealize the direct responsible individuals and say this is

00:41:09.700 --> 00:41:11.695
the person responsible for this set of decisions.

00:41:11.695 --> 00:41:13.705
I guess the clearest, crispest way to do it,

00:41:13.705 --> 00:41:16.015
it sometimes means that things fall in the cracks.

00:41:16.015 --> 00:41:18.115
Did you do a weird study on titles?

00:41:18.115 --> 00:41:21.760
Yeah. We actually did a mischievous study on titles where

00:41:21.760 --> 00:41:26.200
what we did was in this experiment with 82 new venture teams,

00:41:26.200 --> 00:41:28.870
in the treatment condition, we asked, ah,

00:41:28.870 --> 00:41:32.155
all the teams in both conditions with four-person teams,

00:41:32.155 --> 00:41:33.910
they had to build a prototype.

00:41:33.910 --> 00:41:37.630
We had to get an external panel of judges to evaluate the prototype.

00:41:37.630 --> 00:41:39.220
But in the treatment condition,

00:41:39.220 --> 00:41:40.690
all that we asked was,

00:41:40.690 --> 00:41:43.810
we asked people and said come up with a job title

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:47.335
that showcases your unique person on the spot.

00:41:47.335 --> 00:41:49.060
So- and it was extraordinary.

00:41:49.060 --> 00:41:52.495
People came up with titles like guru of cybersecurity,

00:41:52.495 --> 00:41:54.655
defender of the source code,

00:41:54.655 --> 00:41:59.230
chief shmoozer, [LAUGHTER] chief banana, chief instigator.

00:41:59.230 --> 00:42:02.875
And we were kind of quite surprised that at the end of the experiment,

00:42:02.875 --> 00:42:05.845
teams where people chose their own job titles,

00:42:05.845 --> 00:42:08.575
they actually built a prototype faster.

00:42:08.575 --> 00:42:12.820
But most importantly, the prototypes they built, they, at least,

00:42:12.820 --> 00:42:16.375
in the eyes of an external panel of judges were measurably

00:42:16.375 --> 00:42:20.380
superior to the prototype's prepared by the,

00:42:20.380 --> 00:42:23.365
uh, other teams under controlled conditions.

00:42:23.365 --> 00:42:24.055
What?

00:42:24.055 --> 00:42:30.745
So my sort of speculation is the um- and we actually had evidence of this,

00:42:30.745 --> 00:42:34.135
when you ask people to choose their own job titles,

00:42:34.135 --> 00:42:37.780
the first thing is you're actually triggering a sense of ownership.

00:42:37.780 --> 00:42:38.140
Right.

00:42:38.140 --> 00:42:39.490
You chose the title.

00:42:39.490 --> 00:42:42.790
[OVERLAPPING] The- the other equally vital thing

00:42:42.790 --> 00:42:47.080
was in the teams where people chose their own job titles,

00:42:47.080 --> 00:42:50.155
it was made clear who should do what.

00:42:50.155 --> 00:42:52.780
There was no confusion about the division of labor.

00:42:52.780 --> 00:42:56.290
So, you know, uh, people were specialists in an area.

00:42:56.290 --> 00:42:58.960
You kinda went to them. They were supposed to do that.

00:42:58.960 --> 00:43:01.420
You know, there wasn't like hey, this is my issue.

00:43:01.420 --> 00:43:02.725
This is not my issue.

00:43:02.725 --> 00:43:04.210
I'm not, you know, et cetera.

00:43:04.210 --> 00:43:11.185
So knowledge utilization actually unfolded far more efficiently in such teams.

00:43:11.185 --> 00:43:15.970
And in teams where everybody had like cookie cutter titles, it was kinda opaque.

00:43:15.970 --> 00:43:17.860
You just didn't know who was good at what.

00:43:17.860 --> 00:43:18.880
Yup. That makes sense here.

00:43:18.880 --> 00:43:19.585
So problems-

00:43:19.585 --> 00:43:22.000
Because to say that's not my job or not my area,

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:24.130
that's the biggest road block to the organization's structure.

00:43:24.130 --> 00:43:26.500
I mean across everything,

00:43:26.500 --> 00:43:27.985
I think that's the number one thing is like,

00:43:27.985 --> 00:43:29.275
oh, that's not my job.

00:43:29.275 --> 00:43:32.335
Right. Uh, any other question that,

00:43:32.335 --> 00:43:34.450
ah, strikes your fancy, Bob?

00:43:34.450 --> 00:43:36.279
Ah, John-

00:43:36.279 --> 00:43:41.155
Uh, somebody asked about how you build culture in remote work forces which I think is,

00:43:41.155 --> 00:43:42.250
is an important question,

00:43:42.250 --> 00:43:43.480
I think, we're seeing in Silicon Valley.

00:43:43.480 --> 00:43:43.780
Yes.

00:43:43.780 --> 00:43:46.330
People are- people have moved. We're- we're starting development centers everywhere.

