Um, Mozilla was a big distributed workforce because people everywhere,

00:43:50.275 --> 00:43:52.195
kinda 30 different countries on the world.

00:43:52.195 --> 00:43:55.750
Even though they're for 500 people and, um, [NOISE] Yeah.

00:43:55.750 --> 00:43:56.980
I think it's- it's just like anything else.

00:43:56.980 --> 00:44:00.790
You have to decide like how you're gonna communicate through- and you have to test,

00:44:00.790 --> 00:44:03.280
test the fidelity of communications, that's engagement.

00:44:03.280 --> 00:44:05.005
I think you also just have to, um,

00:44:05.005 --> 00:44:08.740
figure out ways to get people to be in-person together often.

00:44:08.740 --> 00:44:12.675
So like the rule I made in Mozilla was- we had enough money where I said, um-

00:44:12.675 --> 00:44:17.305
Anytime you need to get together to work on telling somebody, fly somewhere.

00:44:17.305 --> 00:44:18.630
And I don't care where it is,

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:21.670
just figure out where to be and where it would work for a week and just be there,

00:44:21.670 --> 00:44:23.885
and I'll scream if it gets too expensive.

00:44:23.885 --> 00:44:25.855
But I never, I never complained about it.

00:44:25.855 --> 00:44:28.970
So, very minor thing for those of you interested in the question

00:44:28.970 --> 00:44:32.255
of virtual teams that are geographically dispersed.

00:44:32.255 --> 00:44:35.540
Our wonderful colleague, Pam Hinds in the School of Engineering,

00:44:35.540 --> 00:44:38.390
has actually done very interesting research in

00:44:38.390 --> 00:44:41.210
that area and if I recall her findings, um,

00:44:41.210 --> 00:44:44.090
this goes back to your point again, John,

00:44:44.090 --> 00:44:48.270
what she found was the two things that if

00:44:48.270 --> 00:44:52.805
you've been watched these things geographically dispersed teams could become subcultures.

00:44:52.805 --> 00:44:57.390
Yet in Shanghai, I'm in San Francisco and then you kind of fight about everything.

00:44:57.390 --> 00:44:59.410
The two things that they, you know,

00:44:59.410 --> 00:45:03.750
work with Mark Martinson that they found were very valuable to deal with

00:45:03.750 --> 00:45:08.420
dispersion was amazingly the frequency of communication the [OVERLAPPING].

00:45:08.420 --> 00:45:09.685
Mostly face to face,

00:45:09.685 --> 00:45:11.670
face to face early in the relationship.

00:45:11.670 --> 00:45:15.130
And it's actually frequent spontaneous communication

00:45:15.130 --> 00:45:17.590
as opposed to prescribe communication.

00:45:17.590 --> 00:45:20.570
And the other thing was what they found was that

00:45:20.570 --> 00:45:25.750
this spontaneous communication actually showed up her sense of shared identity.

00:45:25.750 --> 00:45:27.480
Are we taking it as to be multi-modal.

00:45:27.480 --> 00:45:32.890
So, ah, Mozilla use IRC which is sort of it some [inaudible] slack.

00:45:32.890 --> 00:45:34.255
I have mixed feelings about slack,

